@@ -967,6 +969,24 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+A misconfiguration on the service discovery side (for example, an accidentally removed tag filter or an overly broad cloud API / catalog query)
+can make a single `job_name` suddenly discover a huge number of targets. Since `vmagent` scrapes all discovered targets for a job by default,
+this can spike CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag sets a global limit on the number of active scrape targets a single `job_name`
+can have at once, regardless of which [service discovery mechanism(s)](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/) supplied
+the targets for that job. This limit can be overridden on a per-job basis via the `max_scrape_targets` option at
+[scrape_configs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs). By default there is no limit, so existing
+setups aren't affected.
+
+When a job's discovered target count exceeds the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to that number of targets for the job
+instead of crashing, rejecting the whole job, or silently scraping every discovered target. Targets excluded because of the limit are
+reported as `DROPPED (scrape targets limit exceeded)` at the `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page and in the `droppedTargets`
+section of `http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets`, the same way [sharded-away targets](#scraping-big-number-of-targets) are — so it's easy
+to notice which job(s) are currently over the limit and how many targets are being excluded because of it.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
